                <pb facs="0505" n="493" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg493"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.24 Raw Materials/1522: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-02-25">February 25,
                        1941</date>.<lb/>[Received February 25—2:08 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>719. Department’s 537, February 19.<anchor n="47" xml:id="d502-f1"/> Following
                    message from Sir John Hay for Mr. Jesse Jones: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“The decision to permit exports to continue at 100 per centum for the
                            second quarter should ensure very adequate and rapid supplies of rubber
                            for all American requirements. In view of reports still forthcoming of
                            shortage of shipping for supplies already available at Eastern ports,
                            wish to emphasize the importance of all steps being taken on your side
                            to ensure without doubt that shipping will be fully adequate for the
                            prompt transport of all rubber exports for America. Have informed Viles
                            that Committee now decided to accept offer contained in your letter to
                            me 5th November to purchase further 100,000 tons.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
